# LabView users — one `user:hash` per line.
#
# Copy to ./config/passwd next to your compose file (it is mounted at
# /config/passwd, read-only) and append a line per person. Blank lines and lines
# starting with `#` are ignored, and an empty file simply means no password login:
# LabView then serves everything openly, or falls back to OIDC if you configured
# it. The file is re-read when it changes, so adding a user needs no restart.
#
# The format is /etc/shadow's and htpasswd's: the algorithm is the hash's own
# `$id$` prefix. LabView accepts bcrypt — `$2a$`, `$2b$`, `$2y$` — which is what
# every tool below writes. Any other prefix is skipped with a warning naming the
# id, and a plaintext password is never accepted.
#
# Three ways to make a line:
#
#   printf 'the password' | docker exec -i labview /labview hashpw alice
#   printf 'the password' | go run ./cmd/labview hashpw alice   # from a checkout
#   htpasswd -nbB alice '<password>'                            # apache2-utils
#
# The first two read the password from stdin and print only the finished line to
# stdout, so `... >> ./config/passwd` appends exactly one line. They do not prompt:
# suppressing terminal echo needs golang.org/x/term, which the dependency budget does
# not allow, and a pipe keeps the password out of the process table regardless — which
# is the property that matters. `htpasswd` takes the password on the command line,
# where `ps` and your shell history can both see it.
#
# A username may contain letters, digits and `. _ @ -`, up to 64 characters. On a
# duplicate username the first line wins, and the duplicate is warned about.
#
# The cost (the `$12$` below) is how slow verification is, deliberately: ~250ms on a
# homelab CPU, and every sign-in pays it. `hashpw` always mints at 12 and takes no
# flag to change it, because the cost is the verifier's property rather than the
# minting tool's. A line made elsewhere at another cost is still accepted — the cost
# is read back out of the hash, so htpasswd's cheaper default works and simply
# verifies faster.

# A structurally valid line, to show the shape. The hash is of a random string that
# was never recorded, so nobody can sign in as `demo` — an example file with a
# working password in it is a credential that ends up in production by accident.
demo:$2b$12$XHw7RQ.exSaTgJ0khWyZt.k6PCSwn3VjezjxsXipgCVKV/00rkIiK
